.TH SWAP 3
.SH NAME
swap \- memory usage statistics and swap file control
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #¶ /dev

.B /dev/swap
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B swap
device holds a text block giving memory usage statistics:
.IP
.EX
\fIn\fP memory
\fIn\fP pagesize
\fIn\fP kernel
\fIn\fP/\fIm\fP user
\fIn\fP/\fIm\fP swap
\fIa\fP/\fIn\fP/\fIm\fP kernel malloc
\fIa\fP/\fIn\fP/\fIm\fP kernel draw
\fIa\fP/\fIn\fP/\fIm\fP kernel secret
\fIf\fP/\fIh\fP/\fIr\fP free
.EE
.PP
These are total memory (bytes), system page size (bytes),
kernel memory (pages), user memory (pages), swap space (pages),
kernel malloced data (bytes), kernel graphics data (bytes)
and kernel secret data (bytes).
The expression
.IR n / m
indicates
.I n
used out of
.I m
available.
For kernel malloc and kernel draw,
.IR a
indicates the current allocation in bytes.
.PP
9space:
.B free
gives the free user pages
.IR f ,
the pager's high water mark
.I h
(at or below it, a process that needs a page waits for the pager, which
swaps out or, without swap, kills the biggest process it may: not the
host owner's
.B noswap
and
.B nokill
processes,
.IR proc (3):
the file servers, the GPU drivers,
.IR gpu (4),
.IR wsys (4)
and the system's servers
.RI ( dontkill ,
.IR kill (1));
when only those are left it waits for pages instead and says so once;
only
.B noswap
processes may take pages below
.IR h )
and its headroom
.I r
(the pager runs until that many are free).
A program that keeps memory it could give back (the LinuxKPI's page pool
of the GPU drivers) watches it and gives back before the pager runs.
9front has no such line.
These numbers are not blank padded.
.PP
To turn on swapping, write to
.B swap
the textual file descriptor number of a file or device on which to swap.
.PP
Only the hostowner is allowed to enable swapping. The pages written to
the swap file are transparently encrypted by the kernel using a random key.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR memory (8),
.IR swap (8).
